# name: test/sql/cityjson_hollow_solid.test
# description: a hollow Solid (interior shell) read from CityJSON through three_d
# group: [sql]

# End-to-end of the design-doc §7 composition for the capability this stack
# exists to serve: computing the volume of a solid with an interior shell
# (a cavity), where the shell partition the WKB flattens away is recovered from
# the spec §8 `geometry_properties` `shells` key.
#
# Fixture: test/data/hollow_solid.city.json — outer cube [0,4]^3 (V=64) with a
# concentric cavity [1,3]^3 (V=8), the cavity wound opposite the exterior.
# Hollow volume = 64 - 8 = 56.
#
# When the loaded `cityjson` emits spec §8 `geometry_properties` (shells:[6,6]),
# the metadata path recovers two shells and the winding check applies; an older
# `cityjson` that emits no `shells` falls back to one merged shell. Either way
# the volume is 56 — signed-volume subtraction is shell-grouping invariant for a
# disjoint cavity (DESIGN_DOC §8.2). This asserts the value; the shells-driven
# two-shell path and the winding enforcement are pinned CI-independently by
# test/sql/st_3d_hollow_solid.test and test/cpp/test_inner_shell.cpp.
require cityjson

require three_d

# Hollow volume 56, and the solid is closed.
query IT
SELECT ROUND(ST_3DVolume(s), 6), ST_3DIsClosed(s)
FROM (
    SELECT ST_3DFromWKB(geometry, geometry_properties) AS s
    FROM read_cityjson('test/data/hollow_solid.city.json', lod => '2')
    WHERE geometry IS NOT NULL
) q;
----
56.0	true
